## Solution

We are given
$$F(x) = \sum_{n \ge 1} \frac{(-1)^n}{(2n)!}\cdot\frac{x^{6n+1}}{6n+1}.$$

The value of $F^{(66)}(0)$ is determined entirely by the coefficient of $x^{66}$ in the Maclaurin series of $F$, via
$$F^{(66)}(0) = 66!\cdot [x^{66}]\,F(x).$$

So we only need to check whether a term $x^{66}$ appears in the series.

Each term in the series has exponent $6n+1$ for some integer $n \ge 1$. Setting this equal to $66$:
$$6n+1 = 66 \implies 6n = 65 \implies n = \tfrac{65}{6},$$
which is **not an integer**. Therefore no term in the series has exponent $66$.

The exponents that actually occur are $6n+1 = 7, 13, 19, 25, 31, 37, 43, 49, 55, 61, 67, \dots$, and $66$ is absent from this list.

Hence the coefficient of $x^{66}$ in $F(x)$ is $0$, and
$$F^{(66)}(0) = 66! \cdot 0 = 0.$$

